00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,981
Lesson 7.8.

2
00:00:02,361 --> 00:00:03,716
Comparative complexity.

3
00:00:04,817 --> 00:00:06,610
We close the unit by comparing.

4
00:00:06,990 --> 00:00:10,187
We have seen three ways
to find an order: quantum

5
00:00:10,187 --> 00:00:13,841
interference, physical diffusion
and digital collisions.

6
00:00:14,221 --> 00:00:17,000
Each counts different
steps, and a step of one

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,659
does not cost the same
as a step of another.

8
00:00:20,039 --> 00:00:24,282
Comparing seriously requires asking which
physical resource each step consumes.

9
00:00:25,383 --> 00:00:30,611
A quantum step is a gate on a few
qubits; the algorithm uses on the

10
00:00:30,611 --> 00:00:35,760
order of n logical qubits and n³
gates, with n the number of bits.

11
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:40,725
A diffusion step is a simultaneous
average over all nodes:

12
00:00:40,725 --> 00:00:45,230
only O(n²) steps are needed,
but on a network of at least

13
00:00:45,230 --> 00:00:48,787
r nodes read with about
2n bits of precision.

14
00:00:49,167 --> 00:00:53,183
A digital step is a word
operation: cheap, but the best

15
00:00:53,183 --> 00:00:56,687
algorithms need a
subexponential number of them.

16
00:00:57,817 --> 00:00:59,248
Let's put numbers to a

17
00:00:59,248 --> 00:01:03,739
two-thousand-forty-eight-bit number,
as order-of-magnitude estimates.

18
00:01:04,119 --> 00:01:06,427
Shor: about four thousand one hundred

19
00:01:06,427 --> 00:01:09,172
logical qubits and about
nine billion gates.

20
00:01:09,552 --> 00:01:14,268
Diffusion: about seventeen million steps,
but a network of up to two to the two

21
00:01:14,268 --> 00:01:18,508
thousand forty-eight nodes and four
thousand bits of readout precision.

22
00:01:18,888 --> 00:01:21,403
The number field sieve:
on the order of two

23
00:01:21,403 --> 00:01:23,744
to the one hundred seventeen operations.

24
00:01:24,124 --> 00:01:26,701
Pollard's rho: two to
the five hundred twelve.

25
00:01:27,817 --> 00:01:31,466
The lesson is that the difficulty
does not disappear: it moves.

26
00:01:31,846 --> 00:01:34,988
Shor pays for it in quantum
coherence and error correction.

27
00:01:35,368 --> 00:01:38,998
Diffusion pays for it in area,
in number of nodes and in

28
00:01:38,998 --> 00:01:42,693
analog precision, which grow
exponentially with the bits.

29
00:01:43,073 --> 00:01:44,824
Digital pays for it in time.

30
00:01:45,204 --> 00:01:48,842
Interference that amplifies
versus dissipation that erases:

31
00:01:48,842 --> 00:01:53,160
dissipation is cheap per step, but
needs to represent the whole group.

32
00:01:54,283 --> 00:01:56,957
The same comparison
appears in linear algebra.

33
00:01:57,337 --> 00:02:01,549
The quantum HHL algorithm
solves A x = b in a time that

34
00:02:01,549 --> 00:02:05,147
grows only with the
logarithm of the dimension,

35
00:02:05,527 --> 00:02:09,883
but requires a sparse matrix accessible
through an oracle and delivers a

36
00:02:09,883 --> 00:02:14,299
quantum state, not the vector: reading
all entries costs what was gained.

37
00:02:14,679 --> 00:02:20,018
The thermodynamic linear algebra
of unit six delivers the classical

38
00:02:20,018 --> 00:02:24,719
vector, scales as d κ² ε⁻² and
pays for loading the matrix.

39
00:02:25,099 --> 00:02:27,994
Different promises, different fine print.

40
00:02:29,117 --> 00:02:32,535
For any advantage claim, ask four things.

41
00:02:32,915 --> 00:02:35,539
What is a step, and how much
does it cost physically?

42
00:02:35,919 --> 00:02:40,083
How do area, energy and
precision grow with problem size?

43
00:02:40,463 --> 00:02:43,054
What is delivered at the end, and
how much does it cost to read it?

44
00:02:43,434 --> 00:02:47,012
Against which classical algorithm is
it compared, and under what conditions?

45
00:02:47,392 --> 00:02:51,716
With those questions, the results
of this unit fall into place: deep

46
00:02:51,716 --> 00:02:56,041
ideas about the structure of groups,
not shortcuts for cryptography.

47
00:02:57,150 --> 00:03:02,247
Use this comparison to evaluate
claims with explicit models of time,

48
00:03:02,247 --> 00:03:07,194
area, energy and precision, labeling
each number as in lesson 5.8.

49
00:03:07,574 --> 00:03:11,395
In the final project you will include
a resource table like today's.

50
00:03:12,517 --> 00:03:15,188
Common mistakes: comparing step counts of

51
00:03:15,188 --> 00:03:17,925
different models as if they cost the same.

52
00:03:18,305 --> 00:03:21,446
Ignoring that diffusion needs
on the order of r nodes.

53
00:03:21,826 --> 00:03:25,926
Ignoring HHL's input
oracle and quantum output.

54
00:03:26,306 --> 00:03:28,745
And confusing physical
with logical qubits.

55
00:03:29,850 --> 00:03:32,923
In summary: a quantum
step, a diffusion step and

56
00:03:32,923 --> 00:03:35,676
a digital step consume
different resources.

57
00:03:36,056 --> 00:03:39,888
For two thousand forty-eight
bits, Shor uses thousands of

58
00:03:39,888 --> 00:03:44,057
logical qubits, diffusion few
steps but an exponentially large

59
00:03:44,057 --> 00:03:47,755
and precise network, and
digital a subexponential time.

60
00:03:48,135 --> 00:03:50,740
The difficulty moves,
it does not disappear.

61
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:56,677
And the same careful reading applies to
HHL versus thermodynamic linear algebra.

62
00:03:57,783 --> 00:04:01,264
To practice: say which resource
each kind of step consumes.

63
00:04:01,644 --> 00:04:05,168
Compute the diffusion bound for
two thousand forty-eight bits.

64
00:04:05,548 --> 00:04:09,532
Explain why reading HHL's
output cancels its advantage.

65
00:04:09,912 --> 00:04:13,299
And write the four questions
for any advantage claim.
